<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Kate Sheppard</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1848">1848</a>–<a href="/years/1934">1934</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · Also known as Katherine Wilson Sheppard</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Kate Sheppard was a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-zealand">New Zealand</a> suffragist who led the campaign for women's right to vote. Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/liverpool">Liverpool</a>, she emigrated to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/christchurch">Christchurch</a> and in 1887 became national superintendent for franchise and legislation of New Zealand's Women's Christian Temperance Union. She wrote pamphlets and organised three petitions to Parliament between 1891 and 1893, the last signed by 31,872 women, before the Electoral Act of September 1893 made New Zealand the first self-governing country in which women could vote in parliamentary elections. She was the first president of the National Council of Women of New Zealand, founded in 1896.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/kate-sheppard.jpg" alt="Kate Sheppard" width="320" height="448" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: H. H. Clifford · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Mrs._K._W._Sheppard_(cropped).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
